# Trim Old-Driver-Reference Coverage When You Supersede It

`nodejs/tests/_old-driver-reference/` is a frozen snapshot of the legacy
`snowflake-connector-nodejs` test suite, kept only as a migration reference (see
its `README.MD`/`MIGRATION_PLAN.md`). When you add new coverage — a Rust unit
test in `nodejs_bridge`/`sf_core`, or a new TS unit/e2e test — for behavior that
already has a test in that snapshot, do the trim in the **same PR**, not as a
follow-up someone else has to remember.

Before calling a Node.js data-type or behavior change done:

1. **Grep the snapshot** for existing coverage of what you just added, e.g.
   `grep -ril "TIME\|SfTimestamp" nodejs/tests/_old-driver-reference/unit/`.
   `MIGRATION_PLAN.md` may blanket-park an entire directory ("park until after
   beta") — that note can be stale for the specific behavior you just shipped;
   check the actual files, not just the bookkeeping doc.
2. **If your new test is a faithful equivalent**, delete the superseded
   `it()`/`describe()` block from the old-driver-reference file (delete the
   whole file if it empties out — mirrors Step 6/7 of
   `.cursor/commands/migrate-nodejs-test.md`). Leave a one-line comment in the
   old file naming the new file that replaced it, so a reader lands on real
   coverage instead of a dead end:

   ```js
   // TODO:
   // The "create unique names for duplicated column names" describe block that used to live
   // here has been migrated to nodejs/tests/unit/column-names.test.ts and removed from this file.
   ```

3. **If coverage is only partial**, keep the block and name the gap explicitly
   in a comment — cross-reference `nodejs/BehaviorDifferences.yaml` if the gap is
   a real behavior difference, not just an unported test. Never delete a block
   because a *similarly-named* new test exists without checking the assertions
   actually cover the same cases (same format tokens, same boundary values,
   same precision).
4. **Update `MIGRATION_PLAN.md`**: if the file/directory you touched is still
   listed there as blanket-parked, replace that blanket note with a per-file row
   reflecting the new status, matching how every other section of that doc
   already itemizes per file.

Don't silently leave a redundant old-driver-reference test in place just because
it still passes — that's exactly the drift this rule exists to catch: a reviewer
has to notice by hand that two suites assert the same thing, on the off chance
they read both.

